  "strategy": "1. Approach and Rationale:\n\nWe adopt a hierarchical optimization strategy based on the observation that the optimal step function for minimizing the maximum overlap integral exhibits phase transitions, characteristic of many optimization problems in mathematical physics. The core idea is to find a balance between general structural improvements (macroscopic adjustments) and detailed local optimizations (microscopic adjustments) by leveraging principles from signal processing and calculus of variations. The approach involves three key pillars:\n\n   a) Decomposing the optimization problem into a hierarchy of resolutions, from coarse to fine\n   b) Using phase information from signal processing to identify promising regions for improvement\n   c) Implementing custom gradient-based search using both analytical gradients and stochastic methods\n\nThe method systematically explores different candidate families (gaussian-like, boxcar-like, jump discontinuity-like) which represent different physical behaviors. By maintaining separate processing paths for these families and interleaving exploration and exploitation phases, the algorithm avoids local minima while efficiently navigating the complex optimization landscape. This contrasts with the original approach's heavy reliance on gradient-free local search, focusing instead on physically-motivated structural changes and derivative-based optimization.\n\n2. Mathematical Specification:\n\nThe solution is represented by a step function h(x) with N sampling points on [0,2]. We consider three distinct representations that can be smoothly interpolated between:\n\n   a) Piecewise-constant \"boxcar\" representation (discontinuous)\n   b) Linear ramp representation (continuous transitions)\n   c) Sigmoidal transition representation (smooth transitions)\n\nEach representation is parameterized by a discrete set of coefficients that control the locations and intensities of the active intervals. The objective function is defined as:\n\n   C5 = (2/N) * max_{k} [ \u2211_{i,j} h(i)* (1-h(j))(indicator of x_k between i and j) ]\n\nintegrated over dx. The algorithm supports soft constraints via Lagrangian multipliers and hard constraints via projected gradients.\n\n3. Implementation Procedure:\n\nStep 1: Initialization\n   - Use `interpolate_to` to scale the provided parent solution if needed\n   - Initialize three candidate representations:\n        R1: Piecewise-constant (discontinuities)\n        R2: Ramp-linear (continuous transitions)\n        R3: Sigmoidal (smooth transitions)\n   - Each representation is parameterized by M hidden dimensionality components\n\nStep 2: Hierarchical Stages\n   - Stage A (Coarse-to-Fine):\n        A1: Explore coarse resolution (N/20 samples) with global optimization\n        A2: Refine to medium resolution (N/4 samples)\n        A3: Final optimization at full resolution (N samples)\n   - Stage B (Structural Analysis):\n        B1: Compute phase information (Fourier features) to identify structural candidates\n        B2: Cluster parameter space and focus attention on promising phases\n\nStep 3: Local Search\n   - Phase A (Exploration):\n        L1: Stochastic search using Langevin dynamics\n        L2: Evolutionary operations on parameter space\n        L3: Coordinatewise gradient descent with hard constraints\n   - Phase B (Exploitation):\n        M1: Active subspaces identification\n        M2: Gradient projection with multiple time scales\n        M3: Second-order optimization (Hessian-free)\n\nStep 4: Objective Calculation\n   - Use `compute_c5` with proper cross-correlation\n   - Weight terms by their spectral content to emphasize important frequency components\n   - Use exponentially clipped gradients to handle numerical instability\n\nStep 5: Candidate Management\n   - Maintain a portfolio of candidates with different representations\n   - Track phase information to detect transitions\n   - Use continuation methods to escape local optima\n\n4. Search Choices and Variations:\n\nCore Configurations:\n   - Representation Mixture: {0.3*Boxcar + 0.4*Linear + 0.3*Sigmoidal} weighted by cross-spectrum\n   - Resolution Sequence: [[100], [300], [N]] with interpolation factors (sqrt, cubic)\n   - Priority Modes: \n        * Phase-seeking: Focus on improving current phase (default)\n        * Structure-seeking: Virus frequently visited structural configurations\n        * Noise-immune: Prioritize solutions robust to synthetic noise\n\n5. Computation Budget and Robustness:\n\n   - Total Budget Distribution:\n        Coarse-to-Fine Stage: 300s covers 3 resolution levels\n        Phase Analysis: 50s distributed across all stages\n        Structural Refinement: 500s optimized across representations\n        Optimization Loops: 450s with adaptive step halving\n\n   - Termination Criteria:\n        Successive lack of improvement: 500 iterations without progress\n        Phase space volume below threshold\n        Feature count near physical-optimal state\n\n   - Recovery Procedures:\n        Dimensionality reduction for large N (random projection)\n        Gradient clipping at 1000 * max|grad|\n        Temperature annealing for local search\n\n6. Validation and Return Contract:\n\n   Post-Processing Steps:\n   1. Enforce physical constraints (0<=h[i]<=1) via projection\n   2. Verify integral constraint with tolerance 1e-6\n   3. Re-evaluate candidate using compute_c5 with mode=\"full\"\n   4. Generate grid representation and fallback to piecewise interpolation if needed\n\n   Output Metrics:\n   - Coefficients for underlying parameterization (3 coefficients per representation)\n   - Final score with:\n        tolerance < 1e-8 (C5 evaluation)\n        number of samples = N (current resolution)\n        normalized representation\n\n   - Final Checks:\n        Confirm solution consistency across all representations\n        Verify phase transitions occur as expected\n        Re-evaluate worst-case shift in objective function",
